WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.998
Every Pod gets its own IP address.

2
00:00:04.998 --> 00:00:08.175
Here are three hello-devops Pods, on two nodes.

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.073
Say a config file points straight at one of them.

4
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.055
But Pod IPs do not last.

5
00:00:13.055 --> 00:00:17.223
Rollouts, scaling and failures replace Pods all the time.

6
00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:19.198
The new Pod gets a new IP.

7
00:00:19.198 --> 00:00:21.777
The config file now points at nothing.

8
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.584
So never put a Pod IP in a config file.

9
00:00:24.584 --> 00:00:27.432
Put a Service in front of the Pods instead.

10
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:34.711
A Service gives a changing set of Pods one stable virtual IP address, and one DNS name.

11
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:38.747
The API server gives it an address from the Service range.

12
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:43.416
That address is its ClusterIP, and it stays put while Pods come and go.

13
00:00:43.633 --> 00:00:45.960
Other Pods call it on port 80.

14
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.449
The app inside each Pod listens on port 8000.

15
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:53.587
The full name adds the namespace, hello, and the cluster suffix.

16
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:57.445
From the same namespace, the short name hello-devops is enough.

17
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:00.556
How does the Service know which Pods are its own?

18
00:01:00.556 --> 00:01:03.269
With a label selector, just like a Deployment.

19
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.048
It takes every ready Pod labeled app: hello-devops.

20
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:10.943
A Pod with another label, like this client, is left out.

21
00:01:11.667 --> 00:01:14.631
The EndpointSlice controller watches the Pods.

22
00:01:14.631 --> 00:01:19.723
It keeps a list: the IP and port of each matching Pod, and whether it is ready.

23
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:25.209
Whenever a Pod comes, goes, or changes readiness, the controller updates the list.

24
00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:28.256
Say this Pod starts failing its readiness check.

25
00:01:28.256 --> 00:01:31.313
It keeps running, but its entry is marked not ready.

26
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:34.459
The third piece is kube-proxy, on every node.

27
00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:40.507
It watches Services and EndpointSlices, and writes packet rules into the node's Linux kernel.

28
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:47.445
The rules say: traffic for the ClusterIP, port 80, goes to one of these ready Pods, on port 8000.

29
00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:50.005
The Pod that is not ready is skipped.

30
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:53.452
So the ClusterIP lives on no network card.

31
00:01:53.452 --> 00:01:55.751
It exists only as these rules.

32
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:57.304
Now follow one call.

33
00:01:57.304 --> 00:02:00.780
The client sends a request to hello-devops, by name.

34
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:07.001
First, CoreDNS, the cluster's DNS server, turns the name into the ClusterIP.

35
00:02:08.233 --> 00:02:11.147
The packet leaves for the ClusterIP, port 80.

36
00:02:11.147 --> 00:02:14.903
The rules in the kernel of the client's own node catch it.

37
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:20.768
They pick one ready Pod, and send the packet to its IP, on port 8000.

38
00:02:20.768 --> 00:02:22.957
Here, a Pod on the other node.

39
00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:27.412
kube-proxy itself carries nothing.

40
00:02:27.412 --> 00:02:33.188
The kernel picks a Pod on a connection's first packet, and keeps it until the connection closes.

41
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.007
A new connection may land on another Pod.

42
00:02:36.007 --> 00:02:37.851
That is how the load spreads.

43
00:02:40.567 --> 00:02:41.941
Pods come and go.

44
00:02:41.941 --> 00:02:44.527
The name and the ClusterIP stay.

45
00:02:44.733 --> 00:02:51.695
The EndpointSlice tracks the ready Pods, and kube-proxy's rules on every node send each call to one of them.
